<h2>A quarrel that began over a sari</h2>
<p>The FIR (PS Case No. 259 of 2021, Kritynand Nagar police station, Purnia) arose from a dispute the informant traced to his sister wearing his wife&rsquo;s sari. He alleged that members of his wife&rsquo;s family assaulted his father, mother and pregnant sister. The sections invoked spanned Sections 341, 323, 324, 448, 307, 379, 504 and 34 of the IPC — wrongful restraint, hurt, hurt by dangerous weapon, house trespass, attempt to murder, theft, insult and common intention.</p>
<p>The petitioners — five members of the family, led by a woman — said they had been falsely implicated in what was at worst a mutual altercation, pointed out that a co-accused alleged to have assaulted the informant&rsquo;s sister had already been bailed, and placed two decisive facts before the Court: the dispute stood settled, with the informant and his wife leading a peaceful conjugal life, and the petitioners were persons with clean antecedents.</p>

<h2>What the Court ordered</h2>
<div class="flow">
<div class="fstep"><strong>Relief</strong> — anticipatory bail allowed to all five petitioners.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Window</strong> — the protection operates on arrest or surrender before the trial court within six weeks.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Bonds</strong> — Rs. 10,000 each with two sureties of the like amount, to the satisfaction of the trial court.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Conditions</strong> — release subject to the conditions in Section 482(2) BNSS: availability for interrogation, no inducement or threats to witnesses, and no leaving India without leave.</div>
</div>

<h2>Reading the discretion</h2>
<p>The order is brief, as anticipatory bail orders at this stage often are, but the grant rests on a recognisable triad: false-implication defence, settlement, and clean antecedents. Where an FIR names an entire family across the matrimonial divide, courts are alive to the risk of over-implication — the tendency of quarrels between households to produce accused lists longer than the incident itself. A concluded settlement, evidenced by restored family relations, tells the court that custodial interrogation is unlikely to serve any purpose and that liberty pending trial threatens no one.</p>

<div class="tiles">
<div class="tile"><h3>Role scrutiny</h3><p>Omnibus allegations against several family members invite scrutiny of who is said to have done precisely what — especially where the main assault is attributed to a single, already-bailed co-accused.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><h3>Settlement as context</h3><p>Pacification of the underlying dispute is not a defence to the charge, but it recalibrates the risks the bail court weighs: absconding, tampering and recurrence all recede.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><h3>Antecedents</h3><p>Clean antecedents remain the quiet workhorse of pre-arrest bail. An accused with no criminal history, roots in the community and a settled dispute presents little that custody would address.</p></div>
</div>

<div class="note">
<p>Practice point: where a settlement exists, place it on the record concretely — the fact of restored relations, any compromise deed, and the current state of the relationship between the families. Vague assertions of compromise carry little weight; demonstrated pacification, as in this case, can carry the application even against a Section 307 charge.</p>
</div>
